QB.add(13,[{"n":1,"q":"Comment on the report given below: pH 7.17, Na+ 139.7mEq.L, pCO2 25.7mmHg, K+ 5.1mEq.L, pO2 97mm Hg, Blood glucose 334mg. dL, SatO2 99%, HCO3 std 17.4mEq.L, BE (-8.6)","o":[{"t":"Respiratory Acidosis"},{"t":"Respiratory Alkalosis"},{"t":"Metabolic acidosis"},{"t":"Metabolic alkalosis"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Metabolic acidosis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The correct diagnosis is Metabolic Acidosis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"pH 7.17: This is lower than the normal range (7.35-7.45), indicating acidosis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"HCO3 17.4 mEq/L: This is below the normal range (22-26 mEq/L), suggesting a primary metabolic problem."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"pCO2 25.7 mmHg: This is lower than the normal range (35-45 mmHg), indicating a respiratory compensation for metabolic acidosis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Base Excess -8.6: A negative base excess further supports metabolic acidosis."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Respiratory Acidosis : This would typically present with a low pH, elevated pCO2, and normal or elevated HCO3."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Respiratory Alkalosis : This would show a high pH and low pCO2."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Metabolic Alkalosis : This would present with a high pH and elevated HCO3"}]},{"n":2,"q":"A patient presents to the ER with anaphylaxis; which of the following not a part of the clinical criteria to diagnose anaphylaxis? Urticaria, edema of the lips and tongue, and rashes within 2 minutes to 2 hours of contact. Wheeze and respiratory distress Abdominal cramps and vomiting Hypertension","o":[{"t":"1, 2, and 3"},{"t":"1 and 2"},{"t":"2 and 3"},{"t":"3 and 4"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option D - 3 and 4:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Anaphylaxis is a severe, potentially life-threatening allergic reaction that typically occurs rapidly and involves multiple organ systems."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Although rare cases of hypertensive anaphylaxis exist, the typical cardiovascular manifestation of anaphylaxis is hypotension due to vasodilation and fluid shift from the intravascular space to the extravascular space."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Abdominal cramps and vomiting are not commonly associated with anaphylaxis."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options A, B, and C are clinically observed in case of anaphylaxis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Clinical criteria for the diagnosis of anaphylaxis, proposed at the Second National Institute of Allergy and Infectious Disease/Food Allergy and Anaphylaxis Network (NIAID/FAAN)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"symposium, 2005."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Anaphylaxis is highly likely when any one of the following 3 criteria are fulfilled:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Acute onset of an illness (minutes to several hours) with involvement of the skin, mucosal tissue, or both (eg, generalized hives, pruritus or flushing, swollen lips-tongue-uvula) (And at Least One of The Following) Respiratory compromise (eg, dyspnea, wheeze-bronchospasm, stridor, reduced PEF, hypoxemia) Reduced BP or associated symptoms of end-organ dysfunction (eg, hypotonia (collapse], syncope, incontinence)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Respiratory compromise (eg, dyspnea, wheeze-bronchospasm, stridor, reduced PEF, hypoxemia)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Reduced BP or associated symptoms of end-organ dysfunction (eg, hypotonia (collapse], syncope, incontinence)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Two or more of the following that occur rapidly after expo sun; to a likely allergen for that patient (minutes to several hours): Involvement of the skin-mucosal tissue (eg, generalized hives, itch-flush, swollen lips-tongue-uvula) Respiratory compromise (eg, dyspnea, wheeze-bronchospasm, stridor, reduced PEF, hypoxemia) Reduced BP or associated symptoms (eg, hypotonia [collapse], syncope, incontinence)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Involvement of the skin-mucosal tissue (eg, generalized hives, itch-flush, swollen lips-tongue-uvula)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Respiratory compromise (eg, dyspnea, wheeze-bronchospasm, stridor, reduced PEF, hypoxemia)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Reduced BP or associated symptoms (eg, hypotonia [collapse], syncope, incontinence)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Reduced BP after exposure to known allergen for that patient (minutes to several hours): Infants and children: low systolic BP (age specific) or greater than 30% decrease in systolic BP Adults: systolic BP of less than 90 mm Hg or greater than 30% decrease from that person's baseline"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Infants and children: low systolic BP (age specific) or greater than 30% decrease in systolic BP"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Adults: systolic BP of less than 90 mm Hg or greater than 30% decrease from that person's baseline"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Respiratory compromise (eg, dyspnea, wheeze-bronchospasm, stridor, reduced PEF, hypoxemia)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Reduced BP or associated symptoms of end-organ dysfunction (eg, hypotonia (collapse], syncope, incontinence)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Involvement of the skin-mucosal tissue (eg, generalized hives, itch-flush, swollen lips-tongue-uvula)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Respiratory compromise (eg, dyspnea, wheeze-bronchospasm, stridor, reduced PEF, hypoxemia)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Reduced BP or associated symptoms (eg, hypotonia [collapse], syncope, incontinence)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Infants and children: low systolic BP (age specific) or greater than 30% decrease in systolic BP"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Adults: systolic BP of less than 90 mm Hg or greater than 30% decrease from that person's baseline"}]},{"n":3,"q":"A patient who is a tour guide of Mount Everest presented with blisters on her hand as shown in the figure below. She has severe pain and on examination is erythematous and tender. Which of the following is used in the management of this patient?","o":[{"t":"Oxygen"},{"t":"Apply heat to the area and cast it as soon as possible"},{"t":"Pentoxyphylline"},{"t":"NSAID’s"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option D - NSAIDs:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs) can be used to relieve pain and reduce inflammation associated with blisters. They may help alleviate the severe pain experienced by the patient in this case."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The recommended approach includes protecting the blister, keeping it clean, and using pain-relieving measures such as NSAIDs, if necessary."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Oxygen:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Oxygen is not typically used in the management of blisters on the hand."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - \"Apply heat to the area and cast it as soon as possible.\""},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Apply heat to the area and cast it as soon as possible: This option is not recommended for the management of blisters. It is important to avoid applying heat to the blistered area."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Pentoxyphylline:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pentoxyphylline is a medication that can improve blood flow and is sometimes used in the treatment of vascular disorders."}],"qi":[4990]},{"n":4,"q":"In a post-operative patient, after 2 hours of receiving FFP, sudden onset dyspnea, hypoxemia, tachypnea, hypotension, and bilateral pulmonary infiltrates on chest X-ray occur. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"TRALI"},{"t":"TACO"},{"t":"Pulmonary edema"},{"t":"ARDS"},{"t":"Sepsis"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - TRALI (Transfusion-related acute lung injury):"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"TRALI is a severe adverse reaction that can occur within a few hours of FFP transfusion. It is characterized by the sudden onset of respiratory distress, pulmonary edema, and hypotension."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - TACO (Transfusion-associated circulatory overload) - Presents with circulatory overload and pulmonary edema, but not tachypnea."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Pulmonary edema - General term for fluid accumulation in the lungs, but does not explain the specific findings mentioned."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - ARDS (Acute respiratory distress syndrome) - Presents with severe respiratory distress, but the X-ray appearance is different (diffuse alveolar damage)."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Option E - Sepsis - Infection-related, but does not explain the specific findings mentioned."}]},{"n":5,"q":"Which test is the most sensitive for diagnosing Organophosphate poisoning among the following options?","o":[{"t":"Plasma acetylcholinesterase"},{"t":"Organophosphate level in blood"},{"t":"Organophosphate level in plasma"},{"t":"RBC transaminase levels"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Correct option A - Plasma acetylcholinesterase:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Organophosphate poisoning occurs inhibits the enzyme acetylcholinesterase, leading to an accumulation of acetylcholine and excessive cholinergic activity."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The most sensitive test for the diagnosis of organophosphate poisoning is the measurement of plasma acetylcholinesterase levels. Reduced levels of plasma acetylcholinesterase activity indicate organophosphate poisoning."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - (Organophosphate level in blood) is incorrect because the measurement of organophosphate levels in the blood is not the primary diagnostic test for organophosphate poisoning. The clinical effects and acetylcholinesterase activity are more indicative of the poisoning."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - (Organophosphate level in plasma) is incorrect because the measurement of organophosphate levels in the plasma is not the primary diagnostic test for organophosphate poisoning. The clinical effects and acetylcholinesterase activity are more indicative of the poisoning."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - (RBC transaminase levels) is incorrect because RBC transaminase levels are not relevant to the diagnosis of organophosphate poisoning."}]},{"n":6,"q":"Which of the following animal is most likely responsible for biting a 10-year-old child who presented with symptoms of tachycardia, hypertension, tongue fasciculation, hypersalivation, dilated pupils, and mild erythema at the site of the wound? The child recalls that the animal had a curved tail pointing upwards.","o":[{"t":"Sea urchin"},{"t":"Caterpillar"},{"t":"Scorpion"},{"t":"Shellfish"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Scorpion:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Tachycardia, hypertension, tongue fasciculation, hypersalivation, and dilated pupil following a bite are most suggestive of a bite of a scorpion.The venom is clear, colorless and is a hemolytic or neurotoxic, and is an autonomic stimulator that causes the release of large amounts of catecholamines from the adrenals."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Sting from a sea urchin causes granulomatous nodular lesions along with joint pain and dermatitis."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"OptionB - Caterpillar sting presents as pain, itching, and rash."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Shellfish sting usually leaves a purple or red painful mark."}]},{"n":7,"q":"A 55-year-old patient with necrotizing fascitis was admitted to the ER. The patient was tachycardic and hypotensive. He was treated for septic shock. Which of the following is the characteristic feature of refractory septic shock?","o":[{"t":"Shock persisting > 1 hour after IVF administration"},{"t":"Shock persisting > 1 hour after IVF administration and high dose vasopressors"},{"t":"Shock that is refractory to medical treatment"},{"t":"Shock that does not improve despite treatment for 24 hours"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Shock persisting > 1 hour after IVF administration and high-dose vasopressors:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Refractory septic shock is defined as septic shock that does not respond to initial fluid resuscitation and requires high doses of vasopressors to maintain mean arterial pressure."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The specific criteria often include persistence of shock despite adequate fluid resuscitation and high doses of vasopressors."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Shock persisting > 1 hour after IVF administration: This option does not fully define refractory septic shock."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Shock that is refractory to medical treatment: This option is too broad and does not specifically address the criteria of IVF administration and high-dose vasopressors."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Shock that does not improve despite treatment for 24 hours: This option sets a specific time frame of 24 hours, which is not consistent with the definition of refractory septic shock."}]},{"n":8,"q":"Which of the following is not done before drawing blood for arterial blood gas analysis?","o":[{"t":"Allen's test"},{"t":"Rinse the syringe with heparin"},{"t":"Flexion of wrist"},{"t":"Placing needle at 45 degree angle"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Flexion of wrist:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It is important to keep the wrist in a neutral or slightly extended position during arterial puncture."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Allen's test: This test is performed to assess the collateral circulation in the hand before obtaining an arterial blood sample."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Rinse the syringe with heparin: Heparin is commonly used to prevent blood clotting in the syringe and maintain the integrity of the blood sample."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Placing needle at a 45-degree angle: When performing an arterial puncture, the needle is typically inserted at a 45-degree angle to the skin surface."}]},{"n":9,"q":"An elderly diabetic and hypertensive patient was carried to the emergency room in a comatose state. On examination, the blood pressure was 170/100 mmHg and pulse rate was >100/min. Plantar reflex was bilateral extensor. What is the next step to do?","o":[{"t":"Check blood sugar level"},{"t":"Give antihypertensive"},{"t":"IV mannitol"},{"t":"CT brain"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Check blood sugar level:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In the given scenario, the patient is elderly and presents in a comatose state with hypertension and tachycardia. The presence of bilateral extensor plantar reflex suggests significant neurological involvement."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Given the patient's history of diabetes and hypertension, it is important to first check the blood sugar level (Choice A) to evaluate for hypoglycemia."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Give antihypertensive: While the patient's blood pressure is elevated, administering antihypertensive medications (Choice B) without identifying the underlying cause can be potentially harmful."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - IV mannitol: IV mannitol (Choice C) is commonly used in the treatment of raised intracranial pressure or cerebral edema."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - CT brain: A CT scan of the brain (Choice D) is an important diagnostic tool in the evaluation of a comatose patient with neurological findings."}]},{"n":10,"q":"Please match the following medical conditions with their corresponding descriptions: A) Caplan syndrome 1) Initially observed in coal workers B) Asbestosis 2) Presents with a characteristic \"crazy pavement\" appearance C) Mesothelioma 3) Affects the lower lobe of the lungs D) Sarcoidosis 4) Exhibits the presence of pleural effusion\nA) Caplan syndrome 1) Initially observed in coal workers\n2) Presents with a characteristic \"crazy pavement\"\nB) Asbestosis\nappearance\nC) Mesothelioma 3) Affects the lower lobe of the lungs\nD) Sarcoidosis 4) Exhibits the presence of pleural effusion","o":[{"t":"A-1, B-4, C-3, D-2"},{"t":"A-4, B-2, C-3, D-1"},{"t":"A-3, B-4, C-2, D-1"},{"t":"A-2, B-4, C-3, D-1"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The correct match for the given options is:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option - A-1, B-4, C-3, D-2:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"A) Caplan syndrome - 1) Found first in coal worker Caplan syndrome is a condition characterized by the presence of rheumatoid arthritis along with lung nodules or masses. It was initially described in coal miners who were also suffering from rheumatoid arthritis."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Asbestosis - 4) Exhibits the presence of Pleural effusion Asbestosis is a lung disease caused by the inhalation of asbestos fibers. Pleural effusion can be seen in advanced stages of the disease."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Mesothelioma - 3) Affects the lower lobe of the lung Mesothelioma is a type of cancer that primarily affects the mesothelial cells, which line the outer surface of the body's internal organs. While mesothelioma can involve various organs, it often affects the pleura (lining of the lungs)."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Sarcoidosis - 2) Presents with a characteristic \"crazy pavement\" appearance Sarcoidosis is a systemic inflammatory disease that can affect multiple organs, but it most commonly involves the lungs and lymph nodes. The term \"crazy pavement\" is not specifically associated with sarcoidosis. It is a radiographic appearance seen in certain lung conditions, particularly pulmonary alveolar proteinosis, where the lung tissue exhibits a mosaic pattern resembling crazy paving."}]},{"n":11,"q":"What are the most appropriate tests to perform on a 62-year-old male patient with heart failure who is scheduled for a heart transplant and experiences severe respiratory distress four hours after receiving 2 units of whole blood transfusion, and is found to be hypoxemic, tachycardic, and with elevated mean arterial pressure, considering that his renal function test is abnormal and his hemoglobin level is 6gm%? 1. Chest X-ray 2. Brain natriuretic peptide (BNP) level 3. Absolute neutrophil count 4. Leucocyte antibodies 5. Platelets","o":[{"t":"4 and 5"},{"t":"3 and 5"},{"t":"1 and 2"},{"t":"2 only"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - 1 and 2:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The patient developed severe respiratory distress after receiving a blood transfusion in the given scenario. This raises concerns about a possible transfusion reaction."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Chest X-ray: A chest X-ray can help assess the condition of the lungs and identify any signs of pulmonary edema or other respiratory abnormalities that may be causing respiratory distress."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Brain natriuretic peptide (BNP) level: BNP is a hormone released by the heart in response to increased stretching of the cardiac chambers. Elevated BNP levels can indicate cardiac dysfunction, including heart failure."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"These 2 are the best investigations for the above scenario. Therefore, option c is correct."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Options A, B, D"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Leucocyte antibodies: Testing for leucocyte antibodies can help identify potential immune reactions or transfusion-related complications, such as febrile non-hemolytic transfusion reactions or transfusion-related acute lung injury (TRALI)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Platelets: Platelet levels may be assessed to evaluate the patient’s overall blood count and rule out any platelet-related complications or abnormalities contributing to the clinical presentation."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Absolute neutrophil count: This investigation assesses the number of neutrophils in the bloodstream, a type of white blood cell. It helps evaluate the patient’s immune response and determine if there is any evidence of an inflammatory reaction or infection that could be contributing to respiratory distress."}]},{"n":12,"q":"During a scorching summer afternoon, a man, who had been toiling in a field, unexpectedly collapsed. Upon examination, there were no apparent physical indications of dehydration, although his radial pulse was detectable. The doctor determined that the man's serum electrolyte levels were within the normal range. Shockingly, the man's body temperature measured 106 degrees Fahrenheit. Considering these circumstances, which of the following should be administered as an initial form of immediate assistance?","o":[{"t":"Inject epinephrine immediately"},{"t":"Cover with hot damp sheets"},{"t":"Cover with cool damp sheets"},{"t":"Start CPR"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Cover with cool damp sheets:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The above patient seems to be suffering from a heat stroke. There are no signs of dehydration and the body temperature is quite high and the radial pulse is present. The next best step is to cover the body with cool damp sheets."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Epinephrine injection can be fatal for the patient."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Cool and not hot damp cloth is used to cool the body down."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - CPR is not required as a steady radial pulse is perceived and the patient is not in shock."}]},{"n":13,"q":"All of the following is used in the management of breathless patient at a high altitude of 3000 mts except.","o":[{"t":"Intravenous digoxin"},{"t":"Oxygen supplementation"},{"t":"Immediate descent"},{"t":"Acetazolamide"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Correct option A - Intravenous digoxin:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The above case scenario is diagnostic of high altitude pulmonary edema (HAPE)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"IV digoxin is used in the treatment of heart conditions, particularly the treatment of heart failure and certian arrhythmias."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Oxygen supplementation: Oxygen supplementation is a crucial intervention for managing high altitude breathlessness. APE."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Immediate descent: Immediate descent to a lower altitude is the most effective treatment for altitude sickness."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Acetazolamide: Acetazolamide is a medication that helps prevent and treat altitude sickness."}]},{"n":14,"q":"What is the optimal temperature for the water bath recommended for a patient with frostbite?","o":[{"t":"42 degree C"},{"t":"37 degree C"},{"t":"32 degree C"},{"t":"30 degree C"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - 42 degree C:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The correct answer is 42 degree Celsius. When managing frostbite, one of the primary goals is to rewarm the affected tissue gradually."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The recommended temperature for a water bath in the treatment of frostbite is typically set at 42-44 degree Celsius (104-107.6 degree Fahrenheit). This temperature helps to promote vasodilation and improve blood flow to the affected area, aiding in the rewarming process."}]},{"n":15,"q":"A farmer has come with symptoms of pinpoint pupils, excessive salivation, and increased secretion from the body. The most probable diagnosis is","o":[{"t":"Opioid overdose"},{"t":"Organophosphate poisoning"},{"t":"Stroke"},{"t":"Heat stroke"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Organophosphate poisoning:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Miosis pupil or pinpoint pupil, increased salivation, and body secretion are characteristic clinical features of organophosphorus poisoning."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - In opioid posing, the patient may present with pinpoint pupils. But they may not have features of increased salivation or body secretion."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - In stroke, pinpoint pupils are present. But this essentially does not mean the patient had a stroke. Moreover, the patient may present with other neurological symptoms instead of increased body fluid secretion."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - The patient suffers from extreme dehydration in heat stroke instead of increased salivation."}]},{"n":16,"q":"What is the lifelong treatment for individuals who have received a potent prosthetic mitral valve replacement?","o":[{"t":"Aspirin"},{"t":"Clopidogrel"},{"t":"Atorvastatin"},{"t":"Warfarin"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option D - Warfarin:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"All mechanical valves require long-term anticoagulation because they can cause systemic thromboembolism or may develop valve thrombosis or obstruction."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Warfarin helps prevent thrombogenic events by inhibiting the clotting proteins in the blood."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Aspirin acts on blood platelets only. Warfarin, instead, inhibits the circulating blood clotting proteins in the blood."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Clopidogrel also has anti-platelet action like aspirin. Both these drugs act on platelets and not on clotting proteins."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Atorvastatin is an anti-dyslipidemic drug. Patients with prosthetic valve requirements require regular intake of anticoagulants, more than anti-dyslipidemic."}]},{"n":17,"q":"Which of the following is NOT a complication of blood transfusion in a 25-year-old mother who developed acute postpartum hemorrhage (PPH), hypovolemic shock, and underwent major blood transfusion after delivering a baby boy?","o":[{"t":"Hypocalcemia"},{"t":"Hypokalemia"},{"t":"Hypothermia"},{"t":"Hyperkalemia"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Correct Options B - Hypokalemia:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hypokalemia is not seen with blood transfusions. Instead, hyperkalemia occurs due to leakage of potassium from red blood cells in stored blood."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Options A - Hypocalcemia: While blood transfusion can potentially cause citrate-induced hypocalcemia, which is a temporary decrease in calcium levels due to the anticoagulant (citrate) present in stored blood, it is a recognized complication of blood transfusion."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Options C - Hypothermia: In certain situations, such as rapid transfusion or large-volume transfusion, hypothermia can occur as a complication of blood transfusion."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Options D - Hyperkalemia occurs due to leakage of potassium from red blood cells in stored blood"}]}]);
QB.imgs({"4990":"data:image/jpeg;base64,"});